---
source: tests/help.rs
expression: "String::from_utf8(output.stdout).unwrap()"
---
Sort, merge or check lines of text files and standard input, a Rust clone of the macOS sort(1) (BSD
sort 2.3-Apple).

Lines are compared using the locale's collation (byte order when LC_ALL=C, and always on Linux
builds) unless ordering flags say otherwise. With -k, comparisons use the given key fields in order,
falling back to the whole line when all keys compare equal (unless -s or -u). Global ordering flags
apply to every -k key that has no option letters of its own.

A FILE of - means standard input. With no FILE, read standard input.

Usage: rsort [OPTIONS] [FILE]...

Arguments:
  [FILE]...
          Files to sort, merge or check; - or none means stdin

Options:
  -c, --check[=<MODE>]
          Check that the single input (one FILE or stdin) is sorted; report the first disorder and
          exit 1 if not. MODE silent or quiet reports nothing
          
          [possible values: diagnose-first, silent, quiet]

  -C, --check-silent
          Like -c, but report nothing; only the exit code says whether the input is sorted

  -m, --merge
          Merge already-sorted files without sorting them; unsorted input gives undefined order

  -o, --output <FILE>
          Write the result to FILE instead of stdout; FILE may also be an input

  -u, --unique
          Output only the first of each run of lines with equal keys; implies -s. With -c, also fail
          on duplicates

  -s, --stable
          Keep lines with equal keys in input order (disables the whole-line last-resort comparison)

  -b, --ignore-leading-blanks
          Ignore leading blanks when finding key start and end positions

  -d, --dictionary-order
          Compare only blanks and alphanumeric characters

  -f, --ignore-case
          Fold lowercase to uppercase before comparing

  -i, --ignore-nonprinting
          Ignore non-printable characters

  -g, --general-numeric-sort
          Compare as floating-point numbers (accepts exponents, inf, nan)

  -h, --human-numeric-sort
          Compare numbers with SI suffixes, e.g. 2K < 1M

  -M, --month-sort
          Compare as the locale's month abbreviations (JAN < ... < DEC); unknown strings sort first

  -n, --numeric-sort
          Compare by arithmetic value: optional blanks, minus sign, digits and a decimal point

  -R, --random-sort
          Shuffle, keeping lines with equal keys together

  -V, --version-sort
          Compare version numbers within text, e.g. file-1.9 < file-1.10

      --sort <TYPE>
          Sort by TYPE; the same as -g, -h, -M, -n or -R
          
          [possible values: general-numeric, human-numeric, month, numeric, random]

  -r, --reverse
          Reverse the result of comparisons

      --random-source <FILE>
          Seed -R from FILE's contents, so equal seeds give equal output [default: random]

  -k, --key <POS1[,POS2]>
          Sort by a key from POS1 to POS2 (default end of line). POS is F[.C][OPTS], with OPTS from
          bdfghiMnRrV. Repeat for tie-breakers

  -t, --field-separator <CHAR>
          Separate fields with CHAR (\0 for NUL) instead of runs of blanks; each CHAR delimits a
          field

  -z, --zero-terminated
          End lines with NUL instead of newline, on input and output

      --files0-from <FILE>
          Read NUL-separated input file names from FILE (- for stdin) instead of operands

  -S, --buffer-size <SIZE>
          Ignored; accepted for compatibility (rsort sorts in memory)

  -T, --temporary-directory <DIR>
          Ignored; accepted for compatibility (rsort uses no temporary files)

      --parallel <N>
          Ignored; accepted for compatibility

      --batch-size <N>
          Ignored; accepted for compatibility

      --compress-program <PROGRAM>
          Ignored; accepted for compatibility

      --radixsort
          Ignored; accepted for compatibility

      --mergesort
          Ignored; accepted for compatibility

      --qsort
          Ignored; accepted for compatibility

      --heapsort
          Ignored; accepted for compatibility

      --mmap
          Ignored; accepted for compatibility

      --help
          Print help

      --version
          Print version

Examples:
  # Sort a file by byte value
  $ LC_ALL=C rsort names.txt
  # Largest human-readable sizes first
  $ du -sh * | rsort -hr
  # Sort by the third colon-separated field, numerically
  $ rsort -t: -k3,3n /etc/passwd
  # Deduplicate a file in place
  $ rsort -u -o words.txt words.txt
  # Check order silently via the exit code
  $ rsort -C data.txt && echo sorted

Exit codes:
    0  Success
    1  The input is not sorted (-c/-C), or has duplicate keys with -u
    2  Invalid usage, or a file could not be read or written
  130  Interrupted (Ctrl-C)
